  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q12-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "If the length of each side of a square is increased by 2, the area is multiplied by 16.\nFind the length of one side of the original square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{2}{3}$ units.",
      "worked": "Let $x$ units be the original side length, with $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(x+2)^2 &= 16x^2 \\\\\nx^2+4x+4 &= 16x^2 \\\\\n15x^2-4x-4 &= 0 \\\\\n15x^2-10x+6x-4 &= 0 \\\\\n5x(3x-2)+2(3x-2) &= 0 \\\\\n(5x+2)(3x-2) &= 0\n\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null factor law, $x=-\\dfrac{2}{5}$ or $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$.\nReject the negative length. The original side length is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ units.\nCheck: the new side length is $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ units, so the area multiplier is $\\dfrac{(\\frac{8}{3})^2}{(\\frac{2}{3})^2}=16$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
